  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project in Ramallah</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Project in Ramallah</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Dutch Ministry of Foreign Affairs provided core funding for the Office of the Quartet (OQ) between 2019 to 2021. The OQ and therewith this program has had a mandate to increase Palestinian economic and institutional development and empowerment, as support towards achieving a two-state solution. This fully aligns with Dutch policy priorities in the Palestinian Territories and Israel.  While it is fully recognized that the only way to bring the Palestinian-Israeli conflict to an end is through a negotiated final status agreement, it remains essential that progress is made &#x2018;on the ground&#x2019; in order to benefit Palestinians (and indirectly Israeli&#x2019;s) in a manner that will create a more conducive environment for final status negotiations. That is exactly what the OQ has done on a daily basis during this program period. For that, the OQ reports to the Quartet members (the United States, the European Union, the United Nations and Russia) as well as to the main donors to the office, including the Kingdom of the Netherlands, Canada the United Kingdom and New Zealand. The OQ has worked, together with the Israeli&#x2019;s, the Palestinians, the Netherlands and other international partners to better the cooperation between both parties on the themes of water, trade, energy, rule of law and telecom. The OQ managed to translate the most granular, technical level to the strategic and political level to improve access to water, stable energy supplies and bettered the movement of people and goods. Finally, the OQ has played a very constructive role in the technical preparations for the Netherlands led Trilateral meetings on water, energy and trade. The overall engagement with the OQ in this program has been positive and is lauded by the Netherlands, both parties and other donors. The Netherlands will continue cooperation with the OQ in a new program from 2022 to 2025.</narrative>
  </description>
